# Pipeline isolation

Edit one runtime. Leave every other runtime's files untouched. Pipeline tests share one machine lock so several agents can edit different pipelines at the same time and only one of them runs on the GPU.

`favlo serve` is the entry point for inference. The client selects one model through the OpenAI endpoints: `GET /v1/models` lists the registry, and the `model` field on `POST /v1/chat/completions` loads that row's pipeline. Quantize and the other non-serve commands require `favlo --<pipeline>`. A new runtime keeps that flag.

The write set for a runtime is its entry file (`pipelines/metal41/<pipeline>.py`, which sets `PIPELINE` to that stem), its package beside that file, `docs/<pipeline>/`, and tests that import only that package. Flash Next's note is `docs/qwen38-flash-next/`. 27B's note is `docs/qwen38-27b/`.

A new runtime adds those files and one row in the runtime table in `ARCHITECTURE.md`. The command discovers the entry. Do not edit `src/favlo/cli.py` to register it.

Do not edit another pipeline's package, shaders, tests, or docs. Do not add a base class or a kernel that branches on the pipeline. Do not change `src/favlo/` unless the task is the listener, the registry, or the console. Registration loads `sampling.py` from the pipeline that owns the row. Do not pass a sampling card into the registry.

A pipeline's kernels, records, and cache are described in that pipeline's `docs/<pipeline>/` note. Root `ARCHITECTURE.md` is the process and the write set.

## Model switch

One pipeline is resident. A request that names a different folder or a different pipeline closes that runtime before the next one opens. The same folder and the same pipeline stay resident.

`close` returns only after that pipeline's device memory is released. That includes its key/value cache, prefill workspace, decode path, command queue, and open record. A prefix flush may finish first. After `close` returns, the previous runtime holds no Metal buffer, and the next runtime is the one that allocates.

## Media preemption

An image, video, or music pipeline assumes a chat can set `state["preempt"]` while that pipeline holds the GPU. Read the event at each stage boundary, after the Metal command already submitted has finished. On preempt, save the finished stage in a host checkpoint, leave the job `in_progress` with the same id, and return. `close` then releases the device so the chat's model can allocate. The listener calls the same handler again after those chats. The handler loads that checkpoint and continues the unfinished work. Cancel drops the checkpoint and does not resume.

Device buffers do not survive `close`. A value the next stage needs is in the host checkpoint. Each pipeline keeps that checkpoint in its own package.

## Machine lock

Wait until the machine is free before a test command. That includes `pytest`, `kernels`, `quantize`, `complete`, `bf16`, `probe`, `matmul`, `python -m harness`, and `python -m harness.coding`: any command that compiles shaders, dispatches Metal, loads a checkpoint, quantizes, or starts `favlo serve`. Editing the write set does not wait. Reading another pipeline does not.

The machine is busy when `artifacts/pipeline-test.lock` exists and the `pid` in it is still running. A running `favlo serve` is not itself a reason to wait. Take the lock with the smoke script. When the daemon is up and no request is open, the script closes the resident model and holds chat until release. The daemon stays up. Do not kill the server, and do not kill the process that holds the lock.

```text
uv run python -m favlo.smoke_lock acquire --pipeline <stem> --command <name>
```

Exit 0 means this shell holds the lock and the GPU is free. Exit 2 means a request is open: the lock was not taken. Wait and run acquire again. Exit 3 means another live pid holds the lock. Wait. A lock whose `pid` is gone is stale. The next acquire removes it. The `favlo serve` a test starts itself is that test's process. The wait is before the test starts.

Do not create the lock file by hand. Release it when the testing stretch ends, including when the command fails:

```text
uv run python -m favlo.smoke_lock release
```

Release removes the file only when its `pid` is this shell. A chat during the hold receives 503. After release, the next chat loads a model again. The lock is one JSON object and nothing else:

```json
{"pipeline": "qwen38-27b", "pid": 12345, "command": "kernels"}
```

`pipeline` is the flag stem. `pid` is the shell that owns the test. `uv` and `python` are skipped when the script records that pid, so `uv run` names the shell that stays up for the stretch. A nested command from that shell leaves the file in place. `command` is the short name of that test. Do not write a name, home directory, hostname, or device path.

The file lives under `artifacts/`, which is gitignored. Do not commit it. `pid` is the shell that ran acquire, not the short-lived Python process. Run acquire, the test, and release in that same shell.
